PART 3 - writeBatch idempotency: VERIFIED READ-ONLY (no write performed)

================================================================================

AGENTS.md section 5 / CT-1. The mandate forbids testing this with a write, so

this is an SQL + schema inspection plus the existing production evidence.



(a) seraph/store/writer.py:121-125 - the upsert emitted for every entity:

      INSERT INTO <table> (<cols>) VALUES (<$n>)

      ON CONFLICT (<natural key>) DO UPDATE SET <col> = EXCLUDED.<col>, ...

      RETURNING (xmax = 0) AS inserted

    'xmax = 0' is true only for a genuine insert, so a re-run of identical

    rows reports inserted: 0. This is the CT-1 contract.



(b) natural keys vs AGENTS.md section 5, and the PRIMARY KEY backing each:

      E1  intraday_bars    ON CONFLICT (symbol, ts)              PK (symbol, ts)             schema.sql:21

      E3  daily_prices     ON CONFLICT (symbol, date)            PK (symbol, date)           schema.sql:55

      E4  macro_covariates ON CONFLICT (date)                    PK (date)                   schema.sql:70

      E5  tariff_events    ON CONFLICT (event_id)                PK (event_id)               schema.sql:82

      E14 flow_aggregates  ON CONFLICT (date)                    PK (date)                   schema.sql:216

      E6  hawkes_output    ON CONFLICT (scope, scope_id, ts)     PK (scope, scope_id, ts)    schema.sql:98

          (E6 via scripts/write_hawkes_production.py:265 - pillar outputs are

           not IngestBatch entities, so they do not go through _REGISTRY)

      watermarks           ON CONFLICT (source, entity)          PK (source, entity)         schema.sql:238

    Every ON CONFLICT target is backed by a declared PRIMARY KEY. No target

    lacks a unique index, so no upsert can silently degrade to an insert.



(c) the watermark cannot regress on a re-run (writer.py:202-205, :279-282):

      ON CONFLICT (source, entity) DO UPDATE SET

        watermark = GREATEST(ingestion_watermarks.watermark, EXCLUDED.watermark)



(d) the bulk COPY path (writer.py:264-273) stages into a temp table and runs

    the SAME ON CONFLICT upsert with SELECT DISTINCT ON (key), so

    within-batch duplicates cannot double-insert either.



(e) empirical confirmation already on record, on production data -

    docs/evidence/20261012T223359Z-14-hawkes-E6-idempotency.log:

      pre-run count : 473

      [hawkes] E6 rows: 473 -> 473 (inserted 0, updated 10)

      exit_code     : 0

    10 real E6 keys re-written; row count unchanged; inserted 0. CT-1 semantics.



(f) note on tests/contract/test_ct1_ingestion_idempotency.py: it DOES write,

    but only to the namespaced synthetic symbols CT1TESTA/B/C in daily_prices.

    Workstream D did not invoke it in isolation; it ran only as part of the

    mandated full-suite run, and it touches no real market data.



